  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nNorthwesterly flow has overspread the Northland this afternoon,\nwith some isolated cold advection showers moving southeast \nacross portions of the Arrowhead/Iron Range. Per area cameras, \nlight rain is making it to the ground, but with very dry air \nadvecting southward, amounts will be light. Although this \nactivity will remains isolated, there is a chance the showers \nmake their way across the lake to the South Shore over the next \ncouple hours. A stronger shortwave on the back side of the upper\nlevel trough will cross the Canadian border this evening, which\nmight help kick off a few showers over the tip of the \nArrowhead. Again, amounts will be light. \n\nGusty northwest winds will diminish this evening as high\npressure builds into the region. With clearing skies and \ndiminishing winds, expect good radiational cooling conditions \nand for temperatures to cool quickly. Areas across north central\nMN, the Arrowhead, and interior northwest WI are most likely to\ndrop below freezing, with frost expected elsewhere, including \nlocations along the shores of Lake Superior. Headlines remain in\neffect.\n\nSurface high pressure continues eastward Monday, with winds\nturning back to the south and warm advection setting up. Will \nmaintain a dry forecast for Monday afternoon and evening, but \nthere is some support for scattered showers. Not sold on the \nquick moisture return behind the dry airmass moving through \ntonight coupled with weak forcing being able to squeeze out more\nthan 10-15 percent coverage. This happens south of a low \npressure system in southern Canada, with most of the rain \nremaining north of the border. This system will swing a cold \nfront through the Northland Wednesday. After a warmup on \nTuesday, temperatures will be back in the upper 50s and 60s for \nhighs through the work week. Morning lows will be 30s. Another \nwarmup is expected for the weekend, with southerly flow and \npossibly a little rain.",
